{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-dabb45d0-drift-lesson.md", "content": "# Session lesson: demo drift and unsupported transfers

This instance began with a test grid featuring sparse shapes and edge bars. The demos varied widely in dimensions, background colors, and visual content. Some showed only small localized recolorings (for example a 5→6 patch, a few 7→4 cells, or 8→7 cells), while another changed a compact 5-shape to 6; these deltas did not validate any shared ray, stamp, or edge-bar rule for the test. Repeatedly recoloring salient test motifs without an exact cross-demo rule led to incorrect submissions.

Reusable lesson: track each pair's exact changed-cell mask and old→new colors before hypothesizing. Changing dimensions/backgrounds and unrelated local deltas are evidence to reconsider task identity/distribution, not a license to generalize from shared colors or bars. After a miss, drop the unsupported rule rather than make a cosmetic variation. Request another demo only if a specific predicted delta would discriminate surviving hypotheses; otherwise preserve the remaining demo budget.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u059/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-dabb45d0-drift-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u059/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/task-dabb45d0-review.md", "content": "# Review: task-dabb45d0

This stream illustrated a process failure: despite having three demo pairs, actions repeatedly consisted of reference rereads and another demo request rather than an explicit delta ledger. Demo 1 changed only two cells: color 3 at (2,1) and (3,1) became 6; prominent bars and other shapes stayed fixed. Demo 2 changed three cells: the three 7s in row 4, columns 11–13 became 4; all other content stayed fixed. Demo 3 changed four cells: the 8s at (2,17),(2,18),(3,17),(4,18) became background 5; rest unchanged. These edits differ in object/color/geometry, so they do not by themselves establish one generalized transformation. In particular, the test's conspicuous 3, 7, and 8 formations cannot safely be modified by projecting, recoloring, or stamping based on one example.

Reusable lesson: after each pair, enumerate exact changed coordinates and colors immediately; test a measurable selection rule against all demos and unchanged candidates. A prior note about a matching task ID is cautionary context, not a task rule. If current examples do not validate a shared rule, spend a demo only to test a stated distinction; do not take repeated reference reads as progress. A candidate output must replay every known demo exactly before transfer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u059/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/task-dabb45d0-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/speculative-submission-after-demo.md", "content": "# Avoiding speculative submission after a demo

A returned demonstration pair is evidence to analyze, not permission to extrapolate from a salient resemblance. Record the exact changed and unchanged cells, then check whether the rule is uniquely supported for the current test. If an assumed stamp, anchor, orientation, or source-retention rule is not established by the pair, do not submit a guessed grid. Name at least two plausible hypotheses and request another pair only if their predictions differ in a way that can change the test answer. Analyze every newly returned pair before acting again. This is especially important after a failed submission: changing only one guessed coordinate or preserving a different portion of the candidate is not meaningful recovery unless new evidence supports it.
