{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u059/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md", "content": "# Compact-output rejection: reassess representation

A trial on a partitioned grid with 6×4 cells separated by full-width divider rows produced a 3×3 demo output. The initial response incorrectly submitted a speculative 5×5 grid, effectively disregarding the demonstrated output scale and region structure. The next action requested another demo without articulating a discriminating prediction. On future tasks, an output whose dimensions are smaller than the input should immediately trigger compact region mapping: locate separators, count regions, associate regions to output locations, and test marker presence/color/count/position against all observed outputs. After an incorrect submission, reopen task class and output representation before trying again. Request further demos only after naming competing mappings and the specific observation that will distinguish them.

Note: the currently available demo in this case shows 11×14 input and 3×3 output, so infer only a 3×3 relation from it; do not assume a 6×3 output or transfer an unrelated example. The test input must be evaluated under the demonstrated output shape and its own divider structure.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u059/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-recovery", "file": "references/task-29b1b5eb-instance-review.md", "content": "# Task 29b1b5eb: fresh-instance evidence caution

This review involved a new stream instance bearing a task ID that had appeared in historical skill references. The prior note suggested rightward extensions but explicitly warned it was not a validated universal rule. Transferring that hypothesis directly led to unvalidated output; repeated requests were made without an explicit discriminator or a cumulative pair-by-pair delta ledger. The resulting grid submission was reported as an invalid ARC grid, and the attempted retry repeated the same malformed shape.

A fresh task instance requires independent evidence. Treat same-ID references as historical warnings, not transformation rules. For every returned pair, immediately record exact changed coordinates and unaffected candidates, update named hypotheses, and request again only for an observation that separates plausible rules. When invalid-grid feedback arrives, check row count, widths, dimensions, values, and action serialization before making another attempt; keep format diagnosis separate from inference. This session did not establish a reliable transformation for its test, so this reference documents process failures and recovery gates, not a solved output rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u059/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-evidence-recovery/references/task-29b1b5eb-instance-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/failed-guess-reassessment.md", "content": "# Failed-submission reassessment

When a plausible full-grid cleanup or marker-repair guess is incorrect, treat that as evidence against the whole inferred rule, not a cue to make another nearby edit. Reconstruct the exact input/output delta in every demo, including unchanged markers and structures. Separate the observed operation from assumptions about color regions and object boundaries. If multiple rules still fit, name them and request a demo specifically predicted to differ; otherwise submit only a transformation replayed against all available examples. This avoids cascading unsupported guesses and needless penalties.
